<sn><md>### Commenting Using the Preprocessor

Large sections of code can be temporarily **commented out** using the preprocessor directives `#if 0` and `#endif`.
This technique is particularly useful when the code already contains **multi-line comments**, because normal `/* */` comments cannot be nested.

```c
#if 0
/* Starts the "comment"; everything from here is removed by the preprocessor */

/* A large amount of code with multi-line comments */

int foo()
{
    /* lots of code */
    ...

    /* ... some comment describing the if statement ... */

    if (someTest) {
        /* some more comments */
        return 1;
    }

    return 0;
}

#endif /* 0 */

/* Code from here on is "uncommented"
   and is included in the compiled executable */

...
```

**How It Works**

| Directive           | Purpose                                               |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| `#if 0`             | Starts a preprocessor block that will not be compiled |
| `#endif`            | Ends the `#if 0` block                                |
| Code between them   | Removed by the preprocessor                           |
| Code after `#endif` | Compiled normally                                     |

**Key point:** `#if 0 ... #endif` is not technically a C comment. The **preprocessor excludes the enclosed code before compilation**.
</md></sn>